{
  "dom_id": "step-q57097",
  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "What is the missing number in the following equality?\n\n$$\n3\\times 1\\,\\frac{2}{9}=\\frac{\\,\\boxed{}}{3}\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we write $3$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n3=\\frac{3}{1}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe also write $1\\,\\frac{2}{9}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{2}{9}=\\frac{(1\\times 9)+2}{9}=\\frac{11}{9}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo now, we need to solve the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{1}\\times \\frac{11}{9}\n$$\n\nNotice that the first fraction's numerator ($3$) and the second fraction's denominator ($9$) have a common \nfactor of $3.$ \n\nTherefore, we can simplify our problem by swapping the denominators first and then multiplying the resulting fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{1}\\times \\frac{11}{9} & =\\frac{3}{9}\\times \\frac{11}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\times 11 \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\times 11}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing number is $11.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
